    main.js                 wiring

  The load-bearing decision is that [1m[38;5;153mrules.js[39m is pure[0m. It takes the task list, a ledger of already-fired reminders, and
  an explicit [38;5;153mnow[39m, and returns what should fire. It reads no clock, touches no DOM, and has no storage. [38;5;153mengine.js[39m is a
  thin [38;5;153msetInterval[39m that feeds it [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m and writes results to the notification store.

  That split is what makes this testable, and it's why I wanted Vitest. The hard cases here are all pure-function cases:
  a task due in 14 minutes with a 15-minute lead, a task that went overdue while the app was closed, a task whose due
  date was edited after its reminder already fired, two reminders for the same task in one tick. Every one of those is a
  table-driven test against [38;5;153mrules.js[39m with a fixed [38;5;153mnow[39m — no timers, no DOM, no clicking.

  [1mCatch-up on open[0m falls out of this for free: starting the app just calls [38;5;153mrules.js[39m once with the current time. A task
  that went overdue overnight is simply a rule match on first tick. There is no separate "catch-up" code path to get
  wrong, which is the main thing I want to avoid — a second code path for missed reminders is where this kind of system
  usually rots.

  [1mThe ledger[0m ([38;5;153m{taskId, kind, firedAt}[][39m, persisted) is what stops a reminder firing on every 60-second tick forever.
  Editing a task's due date clears its ledger entries so the new deadline re-arms.

  [1mTooling to set up before any code[0m — cheapest now, and it lands in the spec's Global Constraints so every later task
  inherits it:
